# Line endings, decided here rather than by whoever cloned.
#
# core.autocrlf is on by default on Windows, which rewrites every checked-out
# text file to CRLF. For a shell script that is fatal: Linux reads the trailing
# carriage return as part of the interpreter path and answers "bad interpreter:
# /usr/bin/env bash^M". The scripts are the documented way in on Linux and
# macOS, so they must arrive with LF whatever the machine that checked them out
# prefers.
*.sh    text eol=lf

# The batch files are the mirror image: cmd.exe wants CRLF, and a .bat with
# bare LF can mis-execute a multi-line command.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.psm1  text eol=crlf
*.psd1  text eol=crlf

# Everything else follows the working tree, normalised in the repository.
*       text=auto

# Binary, never touched.
*.png   binary
*.gif   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gguf  binary
